WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.334
-Our next guest
is a talented actor

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.634
you know from the show
"The Get Down"

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:05.834
as well as movies like
"Pokémon: Detective Pikachu"

00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:07.334
and the "Jurassic World"
franchise.

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:09.901
He stars in "Sharper,"
which is in select theaters

00:00:09.901 --> 00:00:12.801
February 10th,
and on Apple TV+ February 17th.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:14.267
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:16.100
-I can give you the money.

00:00:17.868 --> 00:00:19.334
-What?
-It's in the bank.

00:00:19.334 --> 00:00:20.434
I can just go make a withdrawal.

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:23.434
You wouldn't have to...rob it.

00:00:23.434 --> 00:00:24.901
-I -- I don't understand.

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:27.534
You live at home.
You work in a bookstore.

00:00:27.534 --> 00:00:29.467
-No, I own a bookstore.

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:30.834
How do you think I manage
to keep it open?

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:32.300
All that place does
is lose money.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.067
-You're saying you have that
money just sitting in a bank?

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.968
-Yeah, more, yeah.

00:00:36.968 --> 00:00:38.734
My father has money.

00:00:38.734 --> 00:00:41.367
-Please welcome to the show
Justice Smith, everybody.

00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:43.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.868 --> 00:00:53.868
♪♪

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:55.901
Thank you so much
for being with us.

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:57.200
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.801
-I know you just got
off the plane from London.

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:01.067
-Yeah, that's what
I was about to say.

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:02.801
Yeah, I'm --
I'm a little lost.

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:04.200
-Okay, good.
We're gonna get you --

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.300
We're gonna bring you right back
into the Eastern time zone.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.667
Because you used to live
in New York.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:10.501
-Yeah, I did. I lived here
for about two years.

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:12.968
I was shooting "The Get Down"
that you mentioned.

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:15.567
And I was staying with --

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:17.601
Oh, actually, there was another
time I was living here

00:01:17.601 --> 00:01:18.801
when I was doing a play.

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:20.367
I lived here
for so many lifetimes.

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:22.267
Yeah, I was doing this play.
I didn't have anywhere to stay.

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.267
My friend was like, "Oh, yeah,
you can stay at my apartment,

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:27.267
but I gotta let you know,
I have roaches.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.100
That's kind of the caveat."

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.334
-It's very nice thing
for somebody to be honest about.

00:01:31.334 --> 00:01:32.667
-Yes, exactly.
And she's like,

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:34.167
but they're mostly
in the bathroom.

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:35.834
So just try to avoid them
in the bathroom."

00:01:35.834 --> 00:01:36.968
And I was like,
"Okay. That's fine.

00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:38.601
I've dealt with worse."

00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:41.200
I get there.
You know, it's a nice place.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.300
There are roaches
in the bathroom.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.234
I kill them all.
I manage to take a shower,

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:45.834
and they just come right back.
So I'm like, "Cool.

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:48.133
I'll take a shower at
my friend's house from now on."

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.400
But living there
for a couple of days,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.167
I just started seeing
more roaches in the kitchen

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:53.667
and in the living room.
And I was like, "Okay.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:56.200
I got to get an actual Airbnb
and get out of here."

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.367
And I go to sleep that night,
that I make that decision,

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:05.067
and at about 5:30, I wake up,
like, almost reflexively.

00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:06.634
I go -- [ Sniffs ]

00:02:06.634 --> 00:02:09.734
And...
[ Audience groaning ]

00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:13.067
I open my eyes,
and I see a long skinny roach

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:15.400
just pop onto the pillow
next to me,

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.868
and I'm like, "Nope!"
and I get out of bed,

00:02:17.868 --> 00:02:19.501
immediately pack my stuff.

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.000
And they were starting
to all swarm.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.934
Like, they felt me leaving.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:25.100
So they were coming out of
the corners of the apartment.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.801
-I'm wondering if
the one in the nose was like,

00:02:26.801 --> 00:02:28.934
"I gotta get outta here."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.934 --> 00:02:30.734
"I heard that guy's
getting a real Airbnb.

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:32.067
I can't live here anymore.
It's full of roaches."

00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:35.234
-Yeah, he was trying
to hitch a ride, yeah.

00:02:35.234 --> 00:02:37.734
-So, in this film,
it's about a bunch of people

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:39.167
who are very good at deception,

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:43.601
using deception
to enrich themselves.

00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:45.467
-Mm-hmm.
-Are you the kind of person --

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:48.534
Are you a good liar?
-No, no.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.067
-Okay.
Do you have tells?

00:02:50.067 --> 00:02:51.934
Is there a thing people can see
in you if you were lying badly?

00:02:51.934 --> 00:02:54.367
-I cry.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:55.501
I laugh.

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:57.200
You know, I laugh a lot
when I'm nervous.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.767
So I'll probably be laughing
throughout this.

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:02.801
But, yeah, when I was a kid,
I was a really good liar.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:04.968
-Uh-huh.
-I would lie about

00:03:04.968 --> 00:03:07.868
all kinds of stuff, just because
I thought it was funny.

00:03:07.868 --> 00:03:09.033
Um...
[ Laughs ]

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:10.868
And that's how I knew
I wanted to be an actor.

00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:15.567
But I remember lying to my mom
that I had broke my arm.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:18.801
And, like, I came in, I'm like,
"Mom, it hurts so bad."

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:20.367
She's like, "What happened?
What happened?"

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:22.567
I'm like, "I don't know, but..."

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:24.167
[ Laughs ]
What did I say?

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:27.033
"We should've saved
15% on health insurance

00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:28.901
by switching to Geico."
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:30.567
It was a popular commercial.
-Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:33.234
-But it's car insurance.
That joke doesn't even work.

00:03:33.234 --> 00:03:34.868
-Yeah.
Well, I think that --

00:03:34.868 --> 00:03:37.367
I gotta be honest --
I bet, being a parent now,

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.400
no matter what
the punch line is, as a parent,

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.701
I'd be pretty bummed if you said
you had a fake broken arm.

00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:43.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.501
-I used to do
all kinds of stuff.

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:47.567
I used to pretend I was sick
by making vomiting noises

00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:50.033
and then dumping my mom's
perfumes in the toilet.

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:52.367
-Oh, wow.
-So you could hear the impact.

00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:55.000
I was a mastermind.
-They must be so happy

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.968
you got out of the house.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:58.701
They were like,
"He's with roaches?

00:03:58.701 --> 00:04:00.834
He's not coming back here."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:03.000
-Yeah, exactly.
-You got to work with some

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.501
incredible actors in this film.

00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:06.634
You've also worked -- You've
also done a lot of CGI work.

00:04:06.634 --> 00:04:08.400
We mentioned "Jurassic World,"

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.534
and we mentioned
"Detective Pikachu."

00:04:10.534 --> 00:04:13.000
A lot of scenes with Pikachu.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.801
What were you actually
acting with in those scenes?

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:18.567
-A lot of puppets.
Like, men in green suits,

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:22.267
like, green body suits,
like, kneeling,

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:23.934
like, operating this puppet.

00:04:23.934 --> 00:04:26.033
But then we would
have to do three takes

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:27.868
for every single scene
that we would do.

00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:30.133
So it would start off
with a puppet,

00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:32.234
and then it would switch to,
like, a ball,

00:04:32.234 --> 00:04:34.133
like, a reference ball.

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:35.801
And then it would switch
to tape marks.

00:04:35.801 --> 00:04:40.234
And so I had to, like, focus
on where Pikachu's eyeline was

00:04:40.234 --> 00:04:43.267
and when he would move,
on which line.

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:45.033
It was really difficult.

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:46.868
But the director made a point

00:04:46.868 --> 00:04:48.701
to try to make us feel
as immersed as possible.

00:04:48.701 --> 00:04:51.601
So a lot of stuff was practical
on both of those movies,

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:54.067
on "Pokémon" and "Jurassic."
But, uh...

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:57.801
-Do you feel, at some point,
it actually helps to

00:04:57.801 --> 00:05:00.267
have that technical side
that it then releases you

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:04.167
to actually act more naturally,
or is it a hindrance?

00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:05.400
-That's a really good question,
Seth.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.501
-Well, don't fold on me now.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.501 --> 00:05:10.167
-Yeah, I don't know.
I'm not sure.

00:05:10.167 --> 00:05:14.467
I -- I feel like
I got a lot of practice of that

00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:17.467
imagining my surroundings
in acting school.

00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:18.734
-Yeah.
-You don't really work off

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:21.200
of people
when you do monologue work.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.067
-Of course, yeah.
-Did you go acting school?

00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:25.434
-No, I didn't, yeah.
-Oh.

00:05:25.434 --> 00:05:27.834
-Which I've been told
in many an audition.

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:30.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.133 --> 00:05:31.901
They say it just like that.
-That's mean.

00:05:31.901 --> 00:05:33.601
-No, they say it like this.
They go,

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:35.601
"You didn't go to acting school,
did you?"

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.100
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.834
-That's so hard.
That sucks.

00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:40.934
-Now, your parents,
both singers, correct?

00:05:40.934 --> 00:05:42.734
-Yes, that's right.
-Now, is this something --

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:45.033
Did they want singing
as a path for you?

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:47.934
-Yeah, that's actually why
my name is what it is,

00:05:47.934 --> 00:05:50.534
'cause my mom thought
I would be a famous singer,

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:52.567
and they would shout,
"We want Justice!

00:05:52.567 --> 00:05:53.667
We want Justice!"...
-Oh, wow.

00:05:53.667 --> 00:05:55.200
-...while they were
waiting for me.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.067
-She was really planning ahead.
-Yeah, she was, she really was.

00:05:58.067 --> 00:06:01.200
But, yeah, growing up
with singers as parents

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.834
was great 'cause there's music
around the house all the time.

00:06:04.834 --> 00:06:08.601
But they also wouldn't let me
sing in the car unless --

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.868
They'd be like,
"Where's your tone?

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:12.567
Use your diaphragm!"
-Oh, wow.

00:06:12.567 --> 00:06:14.133
-I'm like, "This is lame.
I don't want to do this."

00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:15.734
-Yeah.
-So I rebelled,

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:17.467
and I became an actor instead.

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:19.567
-I would imagine
they're somewhat satisfied.

00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:21.234
At least you're in the arts.

00:06:21.234 --> 00:06:23.868
-No, they hate it.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.868 --> 00:06:25.334
-Well, you know what?
They should've let you sing

00:06:25.334 --> 00:06:27.300
in the car.
-Exactly! There you go.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.033
-There you go. Hey, thanks
so much for being here, man.

00:06:29.033 --> 00:06:30.667
It's really a pleasure
to meet you.

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:33.834
That's Justice Smith.
"Sharper" is in select theaters

00:06:33.834 --> 00:06:36.200
February 10th
and Apple TV+ February 17th.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.467
We'll be right back with music
from Regina Spektor.

